\details{
\subsection{Multiple arguments}{

A list of arguments to be passed to the functions being fuzzed can be
provided via the \code{args} argument. If this is left at its default of \code{NULL},
only the first argument of each function is fuzzed.

Internally, \code{args} is expanded into multiple lists, each differing by one
element: each element in the \code{args} list is modified in
turn by each object in \code{what} and the resulting list of arguments is then
passed to each function via \code{do.call()}. Thus, for example, if \code{args} has
length 3 and \code{what} has length 2, in general a total of 6 input lists will
be generated and tested.

If arguments are named, they will be passed with their names to the fuzzed
functions. This allows to fuzz arguments out of order or arguments that are
passed via \code{...}. If a function doesn't have a formal argument of that name
and doesn't accept \code{...}, then the standard R behaviour is to return an
"unused argument" error. This is whitelisted by default in \code{fuzz()}, and the
corresponding result status is set to "OK".

If more arguments are given than the number of formal arguments accepted by
a function, that function will produce a "SKIP" result.
}

\subsection{Fixed arguments}{

It is possible to define arguments that should remain unchanged during
fuzzing by prefixing their names with \code{..}. These arguments will use the
values assigned in \code{args} without modification. For example, to ensure that
the argument \code{na.rm} is always set to \code{TRUE}, it should be specified as
\code{..na.rm = TRUE} in \code{args}.

If all elements in \code{args} are fixed, \code{what} is ignored and all functions in
\code{funs} will be called with the provided \code{args} list. A similar behaviour can
be obtained by specifying no fixed arguments in \code{args} while setting
\code{what = NULL}.
}

\subsection{Parallel execution}{

The implementation uses \code{mirai} as a backend to execute tasks asynchronously
in parallel worker processes. There are two main approaches to control
parallel execution:
\enumerate{
\item Setting the \code{daemons} argument to an integer value (2 by default): this
will start as many daemons as specified (and shut them down automatically
at the end of the fuzz run). Note that there is no benefit in starting
more daemons than the number of available cores.
\item Manually setting up the daemons before the start of the function: this
can be accomplished via \link[mirai:daemons]{mirai::daemons}, which allows to specify remote
daemons as well as local ones. This also avoids the cost of starting and
closing daemons if \code{fuzz()} were to be called multiple times. It remains
responsibility of the user to close the daemons when no longer in use.
When active daemons are found, the \code{daemons} argument will be ignored.
Refer to the original \code{mirai} documentation for a complete description of
its arguments and behaviour.
}

Note that by default, the daemons load the package being fuzzed as installed
when they start. This means that after any corrections are made to the
package, this must be reinstalled before the fuzzer can notice the changes.
However, this may not be the expected behaviour when the package is being
built with \code{devtools}. In this setting, it is recommended to start the
daemons manually, then run \code{mirai::everywhere(devtools::load_all())} after
any changes that need to be tested.
}

\subsection{Whitelisting}{

In order to reduce the number of false positive results produced, this
function applies the following set of rules, to establish if an error or
warning condition should be ignored (whitelisting):
\itemize{
\item If the error or warning message contains the text "is missing, with no
default", which is produced when a missing argument is used without a
value being assigned to it.
\item If the error or warning message contains any of the patterns specified
in \code{ignore_patterns}. In case of multiple warnings being captured, the
result is whitelisted only if \emph{all} warnings match any of the patterns.
\item If a warning is thrown but \code{ignore_warnings = TRUE} is set.
}

If \code{options(CBTF.whitelist.function.name = TRUE)} is set, the following rule
is also applied:
\itemize{
\item If the name of the function appears in the error or warning message, as
it is considered that the condition has been handled by the developer.
}

In all whitelisted cases, the result is "OK", and the message that
was received is stored in the \verb{$msg} field (see the \emph{Value} section).

\emph{Note:} Whitelisting can also be applied post-hoc on the results of a fuzz
run using the \link{whitelist} function.
